-The eviction policy is still open. Candidates are LRU, frequency-based eviction with aging, and 2Q. LRU has little bookkeeping and follows recent work quickly but is vulnerable to sequential scans. Aged frequency counts protect repeatedly popular clips but add counter maintenance and can lag a project switch. 2Q separates first-use entries from reused entries, resisting scans at the cost of extra queues and a queue-size parameter. None changes the external interface, queue concurrency, or disk budget.
+## 2Q Eviction Policy
+
+The cache uses a 2Q (two-queue) eviction strategy to protect comparison sheets from eviction during long sequential screenings. Entries move through two queues based on reuse:
+
+**A1 (first-access queue)**: New entries land here on first cache admission. This queue holds previews that have been accessed exactly once. A1 uses FIFO eviction — the oldest first-access entry is removed when A1 reaches capacity.
+
+**Am (main queue)**: Entries promoted from A1 on second access. This queue holds previews that have been reused at least once. Am uses LRU eviction — the least recently accessed entry is removed when Am reaches capacity.
+
+When admitting a new entry would exceed the 12 GiB limit, evict from A1 first; only evict from Am when A1 is empty. A1 capacity is set to 25% of the total 12 GiB budget (3 GiB), with the remaining 75% (9 GiB) allocated to Am.
+
+The 25/75 split protects the comparison working set (Am) while allowing enough A1 capacity that one-off screening previews don't immediately churn each other out before promotion can happen. A promotion from A1 to Am happens on the second access to an A1 entry, at which point the entry moves to the MRU position in Am.
+
+Eviction tracking (queue membership, access counts, LRU ordering for Am, FIFO ordering for A1) is private to the preview worker. No other component observes or depends on these structures.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|----------|--------|------------------------|-----------|
+| Cache eviction policy | 2Q (two-queue) with 25/75 split | LRU, Frequency-based with aging | 2Q protects comparison sheets (the reused working set in Am) from eviction during long sequential screenings (one-off entries in A1). LRU adapts quickly but sequential scans evict useful comparison sheets. Frequency-based aging protects popular clips but adds counter maintenance and can retain yesterday's favorites too long when editors switch projects. 2Q adds queue bookkeeping and a size parameter to tune, but all changes remain inside the preview worker. |
+| A1/Am capacity split | 25% A1, 75% Am | 50/50, 10/90, 33/67 | 25/75 gives Am enough capacity to hold a typical comparison working set while leaving A1 large enough that screening previews can survive long enough for a second access to trigger promotion. A smaller A1 would cause immediate FIFO churn; a larger A1 would starve Am during mixed workloads. |
